"Feature selection approach using ensemble learning for network anomaly detection - Doreswamy - 2020 - CAAI Transactions on Intelligence Technology - Wiley Online Library (https://ietresearch.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1049/trit.2020.0073)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.9 years ago; Crawled: last week; # Feature selection approach using ensemble learning for network anomaly detection ... (2020), Feature selection approach using ensemble learning for network anomaly detection.\n\nVolume 5, Issue 4 pp. 283-293\n\nResearch Article\n\nOpen Access\n\n# Feature selection approach using ensemble learning for network anomaly detection\n\nDoreswamy, \n\nDoreswamy\n\nDepartment of Computer Science, Mangalore University, Mangalore, India\n\nMohammad Kazim Hooshmand, \n\nCorresponding Author\n\nMohammad Kazim Hooshmand\n\n  * kazimhooshmand@gmail.com\n\nDepartment of Computer Science, Mangalore University, Mangalore, India\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nIbrahim Gad, \n\nIbrahim Gad\n\nDepartment of Computer Science, Mangalore University, Mangalore, India\n\nDoreswamy, \n\nDoreswamy\n\nDepartment of Computer Science, Mangalore University, Mangalore, India\n\nMohammad Kazim Hooshmand, \n\nCorresponding Author\n\nMohammad Kazim Hooshmand\n\n  * kazimhooshmand@gmail.com\n\nDepartment of Computer Science, Mangalore University, Mangalore, India\n\nIbrahim Gad, \n\nIbrahim Gad\n\nDepartment of Computer Science, Mangalore University, Mangalore, India\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nFirst published: 05 November 2020\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1049/trit.2020.0073Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\n## Abstract\n\nFeature selection is essential for prioritising important attributes in data to improve prediction quality in machine learning algorithms. As different selection techniques identify different feature sets, relying on a single method may result in risky decisions. The authors propose an ensemble approach using union and quorum combination techniques with five primary individual selection methods which are analysis of variance, variance threshold, sequential backward search, recursive feature elimination, and least absolute selection and shrinkage operator. The proposed method reduces features in three rounds: (i) discard redundant features using pairwise correlation, (ii) individual methods select their own feature sets independently, and (iii) equalise individual feature sets. The equalised individual feature sets are combined using union and quorum techniques. Both the combined and individual sets are tested for network anomaly detection using random forest, decision tree, K-nearest neighbours, Gaussian Naive Bayes, and logistic regression classifiers. \nFS is a technique, detecting relatively features with higher importance and ignoring the less important ones. The goal is to obtain a superior feature set that can optimise, learning the complexity of a model, and describe the given problem properly with the minimum performance degradation. On the other hand, dimensionality reduction tools, such as principal component analysis (PCA) and t-distributed stochastic neighbour embedding, provide a projection of given features into a lower number of dimensions, but the main difference is that they are incapable to preserve individual feature definition, while FS techniques do [[7], [8]].\n\nIn this paper, we propose a novel FS using an ensemble learning approach, employing some of the commonly used selection techniques from various FS classes. As the selection methods are independently executed, so each one identifies a different feature set. To see the potential power of incorporation of different methods, we combine the results of individual methods using union and quorum based on set theory and compare AD performance of each combined feature sets and individual sets using RF, DT, K-nearest neighbour (KNN), Gaussian Naive Bayes (GNB), and logistic regression (LR) classifiers with publicly available network traffic data sets: UNSW-NB15 and NSL-KDD.\n\nThe paper is structured as follows: Section 2 summarises related works. Section 3 gives a brief explanation of FS methods. Data sets’ description is given in Section 4. Our proposed method is explained in Section 5. Evaluation of the proposed method is given in Section 6, and finally, Section 7 concludes the study.\n\n### Keywords\n\n  * feature selection\n  * decision trees\n  * data mining\n  * pattern classification\n  * security of data\n  * support vector machines\n  * Bayes methods\n  * feature extraction\n  * regression analysis\n  * random forests\n\n  * machine learning algorithms\n  * primary individual selection methods\n  * recursive feature elimination\n  * absolute selection\n  * shrinkage operator\n  * discard redundant features\n  * individual methods\n  * equalise individual feature sets\n  * equalised individual feature sets\n  * quorum techniques\n  * network anomaly detection\n  * random forest\n  * quorum feature\n  * feature selection approach\n  * ensemble learning\n\n### Publication History\n\n  * Issue Online: 05 November 2020\n  * Version of Record online: 05 November 2020\n  * Manuscript accepted: 29 June 2020\n  * Manuscript revised: 09 June 2020\n  * Manuscript received: 13 March 2020\n\n### How to cite\n\nDoreswamy, , Hooshmand, M.K. and Gad, I. (2020), Feature selection approach using ensemble learning for network anomaly detection. CAAI Trans. Intell. Technol., 5: 283-293. https://doi.org/10.1049/trit.2020.0073--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputer Science Review 39 (2021) 100357 (https://scholarworks.unist.ac.kr/bitstream/201301/49260/2/000621106000011.pdf.pdf)\nciteturn6search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Computer Science Review 39 (2021) 100357 ... Ensemble learning for intrusion detection systems: A systematic ... b Department of Industrial Engineering, Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology, Ulsan 44919